; source/self — a program that is about itself.
;
; `<>` is the program, as syntax. std/source supplies the structure and phrases
; nothing; this module is the other half — the wordings that make those shapes
; a self-examination, and the wiring that hands them over.
;
; The discipline is the one that keeps self-reference from being flattery. A
; model asked to review its own program will call it well-structured. So every
; protocol here either asks for something a compliment cannot satisfy — a
; specific line, a named failure, a rewritten form — or hands the verdict to
; something that is not the model.
;
; ── How `<>` is read here, and why it matters ───────────────────────────────
;
; Every macro below puts `<>` in an INTERPOLATION, never in a position that
; runs it — not an arrow's input, and not a square's branch.
; The difference is not stylistic. `(-> <> "describe it")` RUNS the program and
; feeds its answer onward — and since the program contains the macro, it runs
; itself again, and again, until the runtime stops it. Measured before the fix:
; forty-seven model calls and no answer, where the macro documents one call.
;
; `($ "describe it" <>)` reads the program's text and fires once, which is what
; every macro here means and what each of their Cost lines claims. Reading is
; free; running is not.
;
; What none of these can do is APPLY a revision. `<>` is sight, not authority:
; a proposal comes back as text, and a person or a host decides. That boundary
; is deliberate and is not a limitation to be worked around here.
(
  (# std/source)
  (# std/seam)
  (# std/shape)
  (# std/imaginary)

  ; (self-explain) — what does this program do?
  ;
  ; The plainest use, and the one worth having before the others: a reader who
  ; cannot say what a program does cannot say whether it is wrong.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ self-explain ()
    '($ "This is a Rebis program. Say what it does, in one paragraph, in terms of what it will actually cause to happen: which prompts fire, in what order, what flows between them, and what it finally answers. Do not describe the language, and do not praise the design.\n\n"
        <>))

  ; (self-weakness) — the one thing most likely to be wrong.
  ;
  ; Asks for a location, not a judgement. A model asked whether a program is
  ; good says yes; a model asked which form is the weakest has to point.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ self-weakness ()
    '($ "This is a Rebis program. Name the ONE form in it most likely to produce a wrong or useless answer, quote that form exactly, and say what it would do wrong. If you cannot find one, say so plainly rather than inventing a criticism.\n\n"
        <>))

  ; (self-cost) — what will running this actually spend?
  ;
  ; Rebis makes cost readable off the page — one written prompt is one firing,
  ; a `$` is free, a `%` expands one branch — so this is a question with a
  ; checkable answer, which is exactly what makes it worth asking of a model.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ self-cost ()
    '($ "This is a Rebis program. Count the model calls one run of it makes, as a number or a range if a gate decides it. List each firing form and why it fires. A quoted prompt fires; `$`, `?`, `!`, `<>` and `{}` do not; `%` expands exactly one branch.\n\n"
        <>))

  ; (self-revise instruction) — propose a changed version of this program.
  ;
  ;   instruction   what to change, in the caller's words. Interpolated, never
  ;                 fired, so it costs nothing to say.
  ;
  ; Returns SOURCE, and returning it is all it does. Nothing here writes a
  ; file or replaces a running program: `<>` is sight, and applying a revision
  ; needs the isolated bridge and the validation that only a host has.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (self-revise "make the verdict mechanical instead of asking a model")
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ self-revise (instruction)
    '($ "This is a Rebis program. Rewrite it to satisfy this instruction: "
        ,instruction
        ". Reply with the complete new program as Rebis source and nothing else — no explanation, no fences. Change as little as the instruction requires.\n\n"
        <>))

  ; (self-improved judge) — find the weakness, fix that, keep only the fix.
  ;
  ;   judge   mediator over the weakness and the program, deciding what the
  ;           revision should be. A prompt mediator costs one call.
  ;
  ; The assembled loop, and the reason the imaginary space is imported: the
  ; criticism is scaffolding. Left in the record it is a detailed, quotable
  ; account of what is wrong with a program that has since been changed, and
  ; every later recall on the subject ranks it against the revision. The
  ; critique happens in full and is forgotten; the proposal crosses out.
  ;
  ; Cost: the weakness once, the mediation once. The space is free.
  (~ self-improved (judge)
    '{([,judge]
       (self-weakness)
       ($ <>))})

  ; (self-until-accepted work) — revise until a host says stop.
  ;
  ;   work   what each pass produces and the host judges. Fires once per pass.
  ;
  ; std/source's std-until with the one gate worth using here. A model asked
  ; whether its own program is finished says yes, and a loop over the whole
  ; program is exactly the loop where that answer must not be trusted — so the
  ; verdict leaves the program through the seam port and costs no call.
  ;
  ; The host answers 1 to stop. A host that never does has written an
  ; unbounded program, and the expansion budget is the backstop.
  ;
  ; The work is BOUND before the gate. std-gated-by fires its own first
  ; argument inside the condition, so handing it the work twice would draft
  ; twice per pass and gate one draft while returning the other — the same
  ; confound `=` exists to remove.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (self-until-accepted (draft-the-section))
  ;
  ; Cost: per pass, the work once; the verdict is free.
  (~ self-until-accepted (work)
    '(= drafted ,work
        (std-gated-by drafted drafted <>)))

  ; (self-attempted subject) — try a rewrite privately, keep only the lesson.
  ;
  ;   subject   what the program is being improved AT, and the topic the
  ;             lessons accumulate under. Interpolated, never fired.
  ;
  ; One turn of the ratchet. The program writes a new version of itself and
  ; RUNS it; whatever that produces is marked to the imaginary plane and the
  ; boundary crosses nothing — so the record is untouched and the next attempt
  ; can still read what this one found out.
  ;
  ; Note what does NOT move: `<>` is the run's own program, so every attempt
  ; begins from the same reviewed base rather than from an unreviewed
  ; descendant of the last one. What accumulates is the LESSON, not the
  ; mutation. That is the difference between hill-climbing with memory and a
  ; chain of edits nobody looked at, and it is why self-improvement here needs
  ; no rebinding of what the program is.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (self-attempted "summarising incident reports")
  ;
  ; Cost: one call to write the new version, plus whatever running it costs.
  ; The mark and both boundaries are free.
  (~ self-attempted (subject)
    '(std-noted
       (>< ($ "You are rewriting a Rebis program so that it does better at: "
              ,subject
              ". Earlier attempts already established: " (? ,subject)
              ". Do not repeat what they ruled out. Reply with ONLY the "
              "complete Rebis program, no prose and no fences. The program is: "
              <>))))

  ; (self-climbed subject work) — attempt twice, then do the real work.
  ;
  ;   subject   forwarded to both attempts, and the topic they share.
  ;   work      what the program is actually for. Runs last, in the open, so
  ;             its answer IS the run's answer and its evidence is real.
  ;
  ; The assembled shape. Two rehearsals of a better self, neither remembered,
  ; each informed by the last — and then the work, done once, by a program that
  ; has already found out two ways not to do it.
  ;
  ; Two attempts rather than a loop on purpose: a self-rewriting loop with a
  ; model for a stop condition does not terminate on any principle. For a real
  ; loop use std/source's std-until with a mechanical gate, and read
  ; docs/SOURCE.md first.
  ;
  ; Cost: two writes, two runs of what was written, and the work.
  (~ self-climbed (subject work)
    '((self-attempted ,subject)
      (self-attempted ,subject)
      ,work))

  ; (self-recorded) — write down what this program is, durably.
  ;
  ; A program explains itself and the explanation outlives the run, so a later
  ; run's flashback on the subject recalls what the program was rather than
  ; re-deriving it. Keeping is free; the explanation is the one call.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ self-recorded ()
    '(! (std-with-evidence (self-explain)))))
